<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Hibis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of Hibis is an Egyptian temple in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharga-oasis">Kharga Oasis</a> of the Western Desert, dedicated to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. Begun under the Saite kings, it was largely built and decorated under the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, making it the best-preserved temple of the Achaemenid period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Further additions were made under the Thirtieth Dynasty and the Ptolemies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-hibis.jpg" alt="Temple of Hibis" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RolandUnger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HibisComplete.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
